nodecode-snyk is a rewrite of NousResearch's NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins:

  https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-official-plugins
  commit abec9e822a23089943dd1d476a104bf746f19f86
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2025 Nous Research (LICENSE)

The plugin was one mcp.json entry, which launched Snyk's first-party MCP
server on stdio (`npx -y snyk@1.1306.0 mcp -t stdio --profile full`), and one
skill, skills/snyk-security-scan/, whose SKILL.md and references/tools.md
describe thirteen tools and the loop around them. Every one of those tools was
a thin wrapper over one subcommand of the same Snyk CLI, so this rewrite
keeps the tool names and the argument names and runs the subcommands itself,
from the image, through nlk:run-bounded: `snyk code test`, `snyk test`,
`snyk container test`, `snyk iac test`, `snyk sbom test`, `snyk aibom`,
`snyk auth`, `snyk trust -d`, `snyk logout` and `--version`. The skill's
guidance — absolute paths, sign in first, trust one folder, python3 for
Python, ask before scanning a private repository — is the cell's manual topic,
(help :snyk); its per-tool argument reference is each tool's declared
schema. Nothing of the original is vendored, and no Node, npm or npx run is
needed (a `command` of ["npx", "-y", "snyk@1.1306.0"] runs the same pinned
release for an operator with no global install).

Left out, and why: snyk_package_health_check and snyk_breakability_check are
answered by the MCP server from Snyk's package-intelligence HTTP API, which
the CLI exposes no subcommand for; snyk_send_feedback reports issue-count
deltas to Snyk, which the original marks as opt-in telemetry and this rewrite
leaves to the operator. Snyk itself, the Snyk CLI and the scans are Snyk
Ltd's; the license above covers the plugin, not the service.
